WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.279 --> 00:00:06.400
When the App Store began almost a decade ago, things were simple; pay once, use forever.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:11.340
People would happily pay $5 or even $10 for a good game.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.920
And when demand grew for apps, so did supply.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:19.540
More people in the business of making apps meant more competition, and a dangerous race to the bottom.

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:24.840
The average price of a game is about 49¢, and it's been lowering for years.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:28.840
All of this competition has made many switch to freemium, where apps are free to download

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:31.120
with optional premium upgrades.

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:35.420
This would seem a great solution: apps can still make a profit through upgrades,

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:38.660
but we get our apps far, far cheaper.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:40.060
How great is that?

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:43.020
But as it turns out, "cheap" can be quite expensive.

00:00:43.329 --> 00:00:49.378
When the price of apps fell, companies didn't just decide to settle for less money. They got very clever.

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:54.260
Remember: if you aren't paying for a product, chances are you are the product.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:59.820
Instead of paying with dollars and cents; we pay in time, attention and privacy.

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:05.320
The most generous apps cover your screen with ads, praying that you accidentally tap one

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:09.000
in a moment of poor coordination. But many are far less kind.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.940
They manipulate your brain with tricks designed to deceive you for as much profit as possible.

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:16.949
And, in some cases, it's even worse.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:22.820
The company Moviepass recently decided to lower its price to just $10/month.

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:27.460
That's a free movie every day for basically the price of a single ticket.

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:29.400
This should set off your alarms.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:33.960
Moviepass hasn't become a movie charity, something else is happening.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:39.460
Their goal is to collect as much user data as possible, which can be used for marketing purposes

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:41.700
or sold to third parties.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:47.260
So this means Moviepass is no longer a product for consumers, but a product of consumers.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:53.280
Likewise, when people stop paying for apps, they invite developers to find new ways to stay alive.

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:56.020
Even if it means selling their users.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:58.890
But maybe you'd happily sign on this dotted line.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:03.239
Sure, you don't like ads, but at least your apps are free.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:07.080
Sadly, this is only going to get a lot worse.

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:12.860
Developers that resist freemium will keep making less and less money until they go out of business.

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:17.960
The market actively selects for and encourages poor practices

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:20.960
and will keep doing so; getting worse and worse over time.

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:26.840
The good news is that it's not too late. There are things everyone can do to stop this race to the bottom.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:30.780
Firstly, Apple is the all powerful god of the App Store.

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:33.880
They decide who is allowed in and who gets blessed.

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:39.970
By better promoting paid apps, they can encourage that business model, and by allowing a free trial feature,

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:43.630
people would be more likely to try and eventually pay for apps.

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:45.480
Next, developers.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:49.000
In many cases, they change their practices not because they wanted to

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:51.540
but because of demands forced upon them.

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:56.240
The solution is not to silently accept the changes, but to communicate the problem.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:02.680
If you pay for this app, we don't have to exploit you to survive and we can even afford to update it in the future.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:05.320
Lastly, we the consumers are largely to blame.

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:07.190
We need a new mindset.

00:03:07.190 --> 00:03:10.060
Not of always finding the very cheapest app,

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:12.999
but sometimes being willing to pay for apps we want to stick around.

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:16.920
Of course, some apps will and really should stay freemium,

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:20.940
but it has gone too far and we should be trying to reverse this trend.

